so breaking news out of afghanistan  again where the taliban has captured the  northern provincial capital of  kunduz in another significant defeat for  the taliban  taliban for the government i should say  the taliban fighters attacked early on  sunday morning and captured the city  battling street by street during hours  of intense fighting  officials and residents in kunduz are  describing  total chaos so in just three days this  is what's happened the regions we've  marked in red their provincial capitals  have fallen to the taliban zarage in  nimrod's province  kunduz which you've just been hearing  about the government hasn't officially  confirmed this but sources tell us the  cities have been lost  and those two regions in grey only the  taliban is claiming to have taken  those provincial capitals that's  sherbargan in jazan province  and now we're hearing about sarah paul  as well in the  next province across charlotte bella  sent kabul to take us  through the charlotte i said it  yesterday i think it bears repeating  the speed with which this is happening  now kundas  is quite astonishing  it's happening incredibly quickly i  talked to the taliban  even a few weeks ago and they were  surprised themselves at how quickly they  were gaining territory  it really is taking everyone by surprise  about how quickly  these provinces are falling so if i  start with kunduz this is incredibly  significant we talked to a local  journalist who confirmed to us  that kunluz has fallen that there are  taliban in the police compound and the  intelligence compound and the governor's  compound  and the election commission's office  they've taken the prison they've  released prisoners  and that they're flying the taliban flag  in the centre of the city  the government earlier denied that  condors had fallen to the taliban but  the ministry of defence  has just uh put out a statement saying  that they are launching  a clearing operation of the taliban in  the city  to try to push them out fighting has  been ongoing  around the outskirts of condors in  recent weeks but it was this morning  where the taliban pushed into the heart  of the city  they came from multiple directions and  managed to take it  quite swiftly now kunduz is significant  because  of its location it is on the main  highway between if  you're trying to go from north eastern  afghanistan into kabul  you have to go through kunduz and if the  taliban have condors that essentially  cuts off north  eastern afghanistan from kabul it's also  the bread basket  of afghanistan in about 2015 2016  it actually fell to the taliban and it's  the only city to have fallen to the  taliban in the last 20 years if you take  the last week out of the equation  and then it only came back into  government hands because u.s special  forces were deployed  and they managed to get the taliban out  but now  uh u.s special forces aren't on the  ground anymore of course  okay thanks for those updates charlotte  bellis in  kabul also in kabul now abdullah bahir a  lecturer of transitional justice at the  american  university in kabul i don't know if we  would say transitional yet but  something's happening isn't it and the  as we were saying with charlotte there  the speed with which it's happening  is extraordinary but i also wonder  we knew the us and nato were leaving  something like this was always going to  happen  so i guess a very important aspect is  not to hit the panic button too early um  most of the crises that the world has  seen  is people freaking out before the fire  has  actually started um the idea is yes  these are  concerning developments but this is a  tale  a story that has happened before back in  2001 the taliban did hold all of this  territory  and now it's a much more trained  upon forces with uh aerial backing from  the united states uh so there is hope  it's just that  uh right now it's just taking time for  the government to adjust they have never  been um under such an offensive before  um so it only makes sense for them to  lose certain territory only to try and  get it back  so let's just see now what the  government's reaction is going to be to  restore an equilibrium  so you don't think then this is going to  be like a  you know a series of dominoes that keep  falling i i just as i say  you know four potentially four  provincial capitals  in three days does kind of feel like a  panic situation  um yeah so the idea is that  even when you panic sometimes you have  to not show your panic especially not to  the enemy  uh the idea is that um  there has been a significant change as  well with regards to the united states  involvement with  with b52 bombers involved in  two of these provincial capitals um  the idea is urban warfare is going to be  a bit messy it's not going to be as  clear-cut to take back these territories  um but um there there's hope that i  guess the  government just needs a shakeup with  regards to the realities on the ground  and  once a proper military strategy is put  into place  they can start pushing outwards they  have the better equipment they have the  larger  forces they have the better aerial  dominance or the the only  side that has aerial dominance in the  fighting  so hoping this just doesn't replicate  throughout afghanistan  please realize that the afghan defense  forces are overstretched as well there's  a lot of territory that they cannot have  all their troops stationed at at all  times so  um i guess now when they plan as to how  to take these territories back we'll  have to see what  uh the afghan defense forces are capable  of okay  are there other particular places in  afghanistan right now which you think  are particularly vulnerable  the idea is the the  trend of the fighting is going to change  with the changing weather  most of the fighting the taliban have  made  it a point to attack areas that they  previously were not  that strung in 20 years ago so  they tried to clear up the north to a  great extent i think they will start  pushing towards the south  more and more with regards to cities  like  jalalabad are going to be really  important because then that cuts off the  whole south  if that city falls helman is already  facing  very tough fighting and the idea is  these are major cities i guess  keeping hirat out of the taliban's hands  was a major win  but the smaller cities even if they  might not mean much  symbolically they matter the fact that  it makes a headline that four  provincial capitals have fallen in four  days is uh  problematic it hits the morale and  conviction level of the  forces so um let's hope that they start  changing their fortunes uh  sooner before more and more territory  falls abu dhabi is from the  american university in kabul thank you  so much for your time

